# ── Participant page · Unit Summary, five blocks, speaking to the participant ──────────────────────
SUMMARY_P = [
    dict(arc='Awareness — What', title='What Was Established',
         body='You saw that strategy enters an organisation through two human filters: Cognition (Mind) — how individuals interpret what it means — and Emotion (Heart) — how they experience it. When these diverge across a leadership team, the Functional Bias Problem fragments execution into competing interpretations. The unit establishes Five Principles: Strategy Moves Through People; Misalignment Fragments Effort (surfacing as Chinese Whispers, Clarification Debt, Silent Disengagement); Alignment Must Be Designed (supported by the OCEAVL Assessment and the Emotional Climate Assessment); Human Reactions Are Predictable (the SCARF Model — Status, Certainty, Autonomy, Relatedness, Fairness — triggering the same threat circuitry as physical danger); and Execution Must Be Visible in Behaviour (the ACE-IT standard: Accountability, Commitment, Engagement, Integrity, Transparency). The unit then adds the discipline of change management: moving people from current practice to the practice the strategy requires. A change is an event, and the transition is what each affected person goes through afterwards. A change is adopted when it passes the 4 Checks of Mind, Heart, Hands and Habit. Each check has one tool — 3S (Shift, Stake, Step), SCARF, STAT (Skill, Time, Authority, Tools) and ACE-IT — and one job for you as leader: explain, involve, equip, reinforce. A change stalls at the first check it fails.'),
    dict(arc='Intelligence — Why', title='Why It Matters',
         body='You saw that Heart & Mind alignment is the ignition point of strategy execution — the mechanical conversion of strategic potential energy (the document at rest) into strategic kinetic energy (movement, execution, results). Until an individual decides “this matters to me, I am willing to invest energy,” the strategy remains theoretical. Three foundational logics make the stakes operational: (I) Potential vs Kinetic Energy — the organisation does not move until people decide to move it; (II) Compliance vs Commitment — compliance (Mind only) produces visible output short-term but consumes leadership time and is mechanical and brittle, while commitment (Mind + Heart) generates discretionary effort, adaptive problem-solving, and resilience when leaders are not present; (III) the Strategic Payoff — aligned teams act with ownership, coordinate across functions, and free you to advance strategy. A change follows the same logic. Installed, its value is still potential; adopted, the value is earned. The time between the two is the adoption gap, during which the organisation runs two practices at once and the benefits remain unearned.'),
    dict(arc='Extrapolating — Where', title='Where Alignment Appears',
         body='You saw that misalignment concentrates in seven High Flammable Zones identified by the COMPASS framework: Clarity of Strategic Direction, Organisational Alignment, Management Discipline, People & Capability, Allocation of Resources, Sensing & Adaptation, and Systems & Execution. Each domain carries a signal narrative (“Everyone has a different version of what we said”; “We agreed on this — why is it not happening?”; “We say this is a priority but the resources tell a different story”; “The structure is working against the strategy”) and a reflection question that drives early intervention. You rated each from 1 (strong alignment) to 5 (severe misalignment), and a zone may be either a High Flammable (live leverage point) or a Burning Platform (escalated and consuming resources). Mapping where the signals are lit tells you where alignment work earns the highest return. The readiness scan rates each affected group on the 4 Checks for one specific change. The first check that scores 4 or 5 shows where leadership effort for that group starts, and low readiness inside a High Flammable Zone marks where a change is most likely to become a Burning Platform.'),
    dict(arc='Integration — Collective', title='What Was Built',
         body='You saw how your leadership team enters the Converging Zone — where functional interpretations integrate into a shared leadership signal and the team begins operating as a single leadership system. With Collective Intelligence present, direction remains clear across all layers, resources reinforce priorities, systems enable execution, and people move with coordinated effort. Without it, different leaders transmit different interpretations, COMPASS domains drift, and strategic instability spreads invisibly. Each CXO role carries a distinct alignment responsibility mapped to two primary COMPASS activations — the CEO anchors Clarity and Organisational Alignment; CFO/CPO activate Resources; COO/CTO/CRO drive Systems & Execution; CHRO carries People & Capability; CMO/CSO maintain Clarity and Sensing. When any role drops its responsibility, the COMPASS framework loses an anchor and the rest cannot fully converge — Collective Intelligence is the combined signal. Your leadership team leads change with one voice through four commitments, each protecting a check: one message (Mind), one example (Heart), one sequence (Hands) and one owner (Habit).'),
    dict(arc='Application — In Practice', title='What You Applied',
         body='You mapped the transition for two changes your strategy requires: one practice people must start and one they must stop. For each you ran the 4 Checks, named the weakest check and named one leader who answers for adoption. You set out how your role and the two roles you depend on most activate the COMPASS domains, and you tested your map and your table with a colleague. With your group you agreed the COMPASS domain most often underactivated, where alignment is already strong, and the sequence for the changes landing on the same group. Your group’s confirmed outputs feed your team’s Capstone Blueprint. You closed with one 30-day behavioural commitment.'),
]
